{
  "0": "Embedding representation: apply parameter-free RMS normalization to each nonpadding input item embedding, with epsilon=1e-6; keep padding zero and output embeddings unchanged. This may stabilize GRU gate inputs and reduce sensitivity to embedding magnitude, particularly for sparse items. Apply across all four datasets, preserving dimensions, tying, GRU topology, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer. Reproduce normalization in the predictor writer; retain one trained model per category.",
  "1": "Sequence modeling: reduce the existing maximum causal context length to max(1, floor(0.75 * current_maxlen)), retaining the most recent interactions in chronological order and leaving shorter histories intact. Removing older context may reduce stale-interest interference and recurrent computation without the timeout risk of longer sequences. Apply consistently during training and prediction across all four datasets. Preserve embeddings, GRU topology, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer.",
  "2": "Interest aggregation: replace the current readout with 0.8*h_last + 0.2*sum(w_t*h_t), where weights over valid GRU states are proportional to 2^(-distance_from_last/5) and sum to one. The dominant final state preserves immediate intent while the recency-weighted component may stabilize noisy histories. Apply across all four datasets using existing recurrent states within each single model. Preserve history selection, embeddings, backbone, scoring, loss and optimizer; update the predictor writer.",
  "3": "Training strategy: multiply the existing learning rate by 0.7 throughout training, preserving the optimizer type and any scheduler's relative schedule. Smaller updates may reduce overshooting and improve validation ranking, especially in weaker Electronics, without increasing per-epoch computation. Apply across all four datasets. Keep batch size, dropout, weight decay, stopping policy, seed 42, epoch cap 200, model, readout, scoring and loss unchanged; select one checkpoint per category without averaging."
}
